t's XOR/AND)\n- A wire ends up as an intermediate that shouldn't be there\n\nFor detecting these, I need to check:\n1. For each z-wire: is it produced by the correct type of gate?\n2. For each gate: does its output match the expected type for that gate?\n3. For each wire: does it flow to the correct type of consumer?\n\nLet me implement a comprehensive check:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const gate of gates) {\n  const { op, in1, in2, out } = gate;\n  const isZ = /^z\\d+$/.test(out);\n  const isXorY1 = /^[xy]\\d+$/.test(in1);\n  const isXorY2 = /^[xy]\\d+$/.test(in2);\n  \n  // Rule 1: XOR with both inputs being x/y (same bit) → output should NOT be z (except bit 0)\n  if (op === 'XOR' && isXorY1 && isXorY2) {\n    const bit1 = parseInt(in1.slice(1));\n    const bit2 = parseInt(in2.slice(1));\n    if (bit1 === bit2 && bit1 === 0) {\n      // x00 XOR y00 → must output z00\n      if (out !== 'z00') badWires.add(out);\n    } else if (bit1 === bit2 && bit1 > 0) {\n      // xi XOR yi (i>0) → must NOT output z\n      if (isZ) badWires.add(out);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Rule 2: AND with both inputs being x/y (same bit) → output should NOT be z\n  if (op === 'AND' && isXorY1 && isXorY2) {\n    const bit1 = parseInt(in1.slice(1));\n    const bit2 = parseInt(in2.slice(1));\n    if (bit1 === bit2 && isZ) {\n      badWires.add(out);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Rule 3: XOR with non-x/y inputs → should output z\n  if (op === 'XOR' && !isXorY1 && !isXorY2) {\n    if (!isZ) badWires.add(out);\n  }\n  \n  // Rule 4: AND with non-x/y inputs → should NOT output z (it's a propagate)\n  // Actually, this depends. AND of intermediate and carry is propagate (non-z).\n  // But AND of two random wires could produce anything.\n  // Let's skip this and use a different check.\n  \n  // Rule 5: OR → should NOT output z (except highest bit)\n  if (op === 'OR' && isZ) {\n    const bit = parseInt(out.slice(1));\n    if (bit < maxBit) badWires.add(out);\n  }\n  \n  // Rule 6: z_i should be produced by XOR (i == 0