ut z01 isn't one of the swapped wires. The swapped wires are t01 and b01. Flagging z01 doesn't help directly.\n\nHowever, if I also check Rule 3 for AND outputs:\n- Gate F outputs t01 (changed from b01). Gate F is AND. t01 is consumed by Gate E (XOR). → AND output feeding XOR → Flag t01! ✓\n- Gate C outputs b01 (changed from t01). Gate C is XOR. b01 is consumed by Gate G (OR). XOR output feeding OR → this is fine for carry computation, but... b01 is the output of xi XOR yi and should feed into XOR and AND, not OR. \n  - Actually, is there a rule that says \"XOR output (non-z, from x/y inputs) should feed into XOR and AND gates\"? If so, b01 feeds into OR → VIOLATION. Flag b01! ✓\n\nLet me add:\n- Rule 5: Output of an XOR gate (non-z, from x/y inputs) should be consumed by XOR and AND gates (of the next level), NOT by OR gates.\n- Rule 6: Output of an AND gate (non-z, from x/y inputs) should be consumed by OR gates, NOT by XOR gates.\n\nWith these rules, the t01 ↔ b01 swap would be caught:\n- b01 (from XOR) consumed by OR (Gate G) → Rule 5 violation. Flag b01. ✓\n- t01 (from AND) consumed by XOR (Gate E) → Rule 6 violation. Flag t01. ✓\n\nGreat! So with these extended rules, I can catch all swaps.\n\nLet me formulate the full set of rules:\n\n**Rules for detecting swapped wires in a ripple-carry adder:**\n\n1. **z-wire producer rule**: Any z-wire (except the highest bit's z-wire) MUST be produced by an XOR gate.\n2. **AND output rule**: Output of an AND gate (with x/y inputs, not bit 0) must NOT be a z-wire.\n3. **OR output rule**: Output of an OR gate must NOT be a z-wire (except possibly the highest carry).\n4. **XOR x/y output not z**: XOR gate with x/y inputs (same bit, not bit 0) must NOT output a z-wire.\n5. **XOR consumption rule**: Output of an XOR gate (with x/y inputs, not bit 0) must be consumed by exactly one XOR gate and one AND gate (NOT an OR gate).\n6. **AND consumption rule**: Output of an AND gate (with x/y inputs, same bit) must be consumed by an O